7. Architecture Decision Summary (ADRs)
Decision	Selection	Justification based on PRD
Compute Architecture	Modular Monolith	The PRD describes a core, tightly coupled business process (Education + Attendance). Microservices would introduce unnecessary network latency, complex distributed transactions, and cost overhead.
Database Type	PostgreSQL (Relational)	PRD mentioned Firebase, but hierarchical academic data (Institutions -> Depts -> Classes -> Subjects -> Students) and complex aggregation (Attendance %) are perfectly suited for SQL. RLS in Postgres makes multi-tenancy highly secure.
Identity Provider	Firebase Auth	Mentioned in PRD. Provides free tier, seamless SSO integration, and secure JWT generation without building custom auth flows.
Caching Strategy	Redis	API responses for "Student Dashboard" and "Class Roster" are read-heavy. Caching these reduces DB load and fits the "Performance Constraints" of the PRD.
Asynchronous Processing	Celery + Redis	Generating monthly PDF reports for an entire college or sending bulk emails will cause API timeouts if done synchronously. Offloading to background workers ensures a snappy user interface.
8. Architecture Evolution Roadmap
To ensure the system scales as Attendora acquires more institutional clients:

Phase 1 (MVP - Current Design): Single Modular Monolith, Shared Postgres Database, UI served directly or via simple CDN. Focus on product-market fit.

Phase 2 (Growth): Implement Read-Replicas for the database (route reporting queries to the replica). Implement strict rate limiting per tenant.

Phase 3 (Scale): If a specific domain (e.g., Report Generation or Notifications) becomes a bottleneck, extract only that domain into a separate microservice. Introduce database sharding (e.g., one database per geographic region or tier-1 enterprise customer).

